\section*{Soft Phases in Two-Dimensional O(N) Models}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a mathematical result relevant to this question in the selected manuscript. Square-lattice classical $O(n)$ spin correlations for $n\geq3$ are claimed to decay exponentially at every finite positive inverse temperature.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Rotation identities and multiscale estimates force a quantitative loss of effective stiffness. Once a suitable coarse scale is reached, block estimates give exponential transmission bounds uniformly for free finite graphs and bounded nonnegative couplings.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The main bound and plane-limit corollary establish exponential two-point decay at every finite beta for every n>=3, answering the first question and ruling out a positive-temperature critical point in that free-boundary convention. The bundled low-temperature perturbative-expansion assertion is not proved in this paper. This entry records partial support; complete coverage of the question is not established by this catalogue.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 215, the selected theorem gives exponential finite-free-graph $O(n)$ spin decay for $n\geq3$ and bounded ferromagnetic edge strengths. Plane limits, full $O(4)$ spectral gaps, and continuum $O(3)$ results are beyond that entry. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{ClassicalON.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.ClassicalON.main}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

